# Computer in the Past, Present and Future

## Introduction

Computer is an electronic device, which processes the input data and stores the information in it producing the relevant output. Computer has evolved significantly over the years, transforming from a heavy device with moderate performance to a relatively small, fast and powerful device. It has become an inseparable part of education, business, communication, healthcare, science, media and entertainment.

Computer in the Past

The early computers were big and costly. Some of them required a lot of electricity as they used to take up whole rooms. The first electronic computers were built with the help of vacuum tubes, which were later replaced by transistors and integrated circuits (IC). The invention of microprocessor enabled the development of more advanced personal computers.

Compared to the modern computers, the past computers had lower performance, less memory and storage, and were generally slower.

Computer in the Present

Nowadays, computers are multifunctional devices which come in different sizes and shapes. Some of them can be carried from one place to another while others are usually fixed in a specific location.

 Processors

The central processing unit (CPU) of modern computers is a powerful processor enabling a broad range of applications from simple word-processing software to complex scientific calculations. Modern processors are characterized by multiple cores capable of performing tens or hundreds of billions operations per second. Today, we can find Intel Core, Intel Core Ultra family of processors, AMD Ryzen and Apple M-series and ARM processors among the wide variety of computer processors.

Modern computers also utilize graphics processing units (GPUs) which are generally used for graphic and video editing, playing video games and other graphic-intensive applications. The general-purpose processors or neural processing units (NPUs) are being widely adopted to perform artificial intelligence (AI) processing.

 Size and Design

Thanks to the invention of new semiconductors, modern computers have become smaller in size but more powerful than ever. One of the most powerful supercomputers of today, for instance, can fit in a relatively small laptop or even a smartphone. Today, one can find relatively small personal computers at affordable prices.

 Memory and Storage

Modern computers also have larger memory (RAM), and storage (HDD or SSD). Nowadays, many personal computers have 8 GB, 16 GB, 32 GB or even more of random access memory. In addition, personal computers nowadays have larger storage capacities varying from 256 GB, 512 GB, 1 TB, and above. Recently, solid-state drives (SSD) have become extremely popular among computer users due to their reliability, speed and other benefits. Moreover, one can also store data in the cloud and gain access to it from any device.

 Artificial Intelligence

Artificial intelligence is generally perceived as a powerful tool that can be employed in various fields, including natural language processing, data science, and computer vision, among others. New computers are coming with enhanced artificial intelligence features such as natural language processing or speech recognition, which can be used in a variety of applications and computer programs.

Connectivity.

Modern computers are highly competitive and multifunctional devices, which generally operate faster, transfer more data, store more information and are more interconnected than ever. Thanks to modern networks, one can operate and access various services using cloud computing.

 Computer in the Future.

Computer scientists all over the world are constantly working on enhancing computer performance, and other technological processes making them powerful yet compact devices. Future computers are predicted to be more efficient, powerful, and compact compared to the present computers. Some of them are even capable of operating with less electricity.

Future computers may be able to understand natural language more effectively, assist users with complex tasks, analyze large amounts of information, and automatically optimize system performance. AI could also make computers easier to operate by allowing users to interact with them using speech, images, and natural language.

The future processors are expected to have an efficient CPU, GPU and artificial intelligence. Artificial intelligence is projected to be a standard component of every computer enabling it to understand and operate a wide range of activities. Future computers are also supposed to have enhanced and more reliable internet connections.

The concept of future computers may also considerably change the way we perceive the word ‘computer’. For example, computers may become a part of a broader ecosystem known as the Internet of Things (IoT). Rather than being independent devices on their own, computers may be embedded in other devices such as cars, home appliances and other equipment.

Another concept, which may change the future of computers, is quantum computing. Unlike the present computers, which mainly operate on bits capable of holding either 1 or 0, quantum computers are supposed to utilize qubits, which have the ability to hold both 1 and 0 at the same time, which will lead to faster computations. Future computers are also expected to be more interconnected and employ more practical applications such as voice recognition, gesture control, augmented reality, virtual reality and other emerging applications. However, reducing the global energy consumption will also become even more significant.

 Conclusion

Computers have come a long way evolving from being large machines with limited performance to multifunctional and powerful devices, which are an inseparable part of our everyday lives. The modern computers provide high performance, large memory, advanced graphic cards, artificial intelligence and other recent technologies. In the future, computers are projected to become more efficient, powerful and compact with better processors, artificial intelligence, quantum computing and more practical applications.
